`python3 test_all.py` now prints `ALL PASS`. I fixed four bugs across the three source files and left `test_all.py` untouched.

- **`mean`** (`stats.py`): divided by `len(xs) - 1` instead of `len(xs)`. This also corrected `variance`, which depends on it.
- **`median`** (`stats.py`): the even-length case returned the upper middle element instead of the average of the two middle ones.
- **`dedup`** (`seq.py`): the logic was inverted, so it kept only the duplicates rather than the first occurrences.
- **`top_words`** (`textproc.py`): ranked words alphabetically rather than by count. It now sorts by count descending, with ties broken alphabetically (the tie-break is my choice).

The suite went green after the third fix, before I touched `top_words`. Its only test uses `"x x x y y z"`, where alphabetical order and count order happen to coincide, so the test cannot catch that bug. I fixed it anyway and spot-checked it: `top_words('a b b c c c', 2)` now gives `['c', 'b']`. A test input where the two orders differ would be worth adding.

Nothing is committed; the changes are in the working tree, along with an untracked `__pycache__/` from the test runs.
